menu "Mesh module SDK"

    config MESH_AO_PRIORITY
        int "Priority of the module's owner (0: KODE_PRIO_USER_HIGH)"
        range 0 24
        default 0
        help
            The QP/C priority of the Active Object that owns the Mesh
            module, which is also its task's FreeRTOS priority. 0 takes
            kode-sdk's KODE_PRIO_USER_HIGH; mesh_ao.c says why. Two Active
            Objects at one level stop the board when the second starts, so
            an app whose own Active Object needs KODE_PRIO_USER_HIGH moves
            this one to a level nothing in its image holds.

    config MESH_LAB
        bool "Lift the region's limits: power, frequency, packet, duty cycle, test signal"
        default n
        help
            mesh_region.h holds every transmit of this SDK to the EU plan.
            Off, the default:
            mesh_radio_begin() and mesh_radio_configure() hold the power to the
            cap (8.5 dBm nominal on 868 MHz and 4.5 dBm on 2.4 GHz), and
            mesh_radio_configure() refuses anything but the band's own
            bandwidth inside the band's edges, SF7 to SF12, coding rate 4/5,
            eight preamble symbols and the private sync word.

            On, these are lifted, and only these:
             - the power, walking the default amplifier row as far as table
               7-19 or 7-21 gives it: 19 dBm on the LF amplifier and 10 dBm on
               the HF one. Above that, up to the chip's own top (+22 dBm and
               +12 dBm, datasheet 7.4), only with MESH_LAB_PA_TABLE: the tables'
               rows there are Semtech's reference design at 915 and 2445 MHz;
               use them at your own risk;
             - the frequency, over the whole of each path: 150 to 960 MHz and
               1500 to 2500 MHz, whatever the module's antennas are for;
             - with mesh_radio_configure(), any bandwidth of the chip, SF5 to
               SF12, coding rate 4/5 to 4/8, any preamble and any sync word
               byte (the datasheet lists 0x12 and 0x34 as the valid ones);
             - with mesh_radio_configure(), the SDK's count of the 1 % duty
               cycle: an app then keeps its region's duty cycle itself.
               mesh_radio_begin() is still counted;
             - the test signal, mesh_radio_test(): a carrier or a bare
               preamble, on air for as long as it is asked and never counted
               in the duty cycle. Off, the call is refused and nothing is
               keyed.

            A test signal (mesh_radio_test) is still bounded in time and still
            goes into a 50 ohm load only; the over-current thresholds are
            unchanged; and until the app confirms the antennas
            (mesh_radio_antennas_confirm), every transmit is still held to
            MESH_SAFE_POWER_CDBM, as in any build. This is a build for testing
            the module, not for a firmware that is distributed: it puts on air
            whatever power, band and duty cycle its code asks for. Before any
            transmit, an antenna or a load on that band's SMA.

    config MESH_LAB_PA_TABLE
        bool "With MESH_LAB, drive the amplifier with the sheet's rows above the default one"
        depends on MESH_LAB
        default n
        help
            Rows of the datasheet's power tables (7-19 at 915 MHz, 7-21 at
            2445 MHz) that this SDK uses above the default row (19 dBm on the
            LF amplifier, 10 dBm on the HF one): the datasheet's rows, for
            Semtech's reference design. Use them at your own risk: they can
            damage the amplifier.

            Off, the default, MESH_LAB holds the power to the top of the
            default row, 19 dBm on LF and 10 dBm on HF: an ask above is
            given that. On, the rows above are used, up to +22 dBm and +12 dBm.
            A non-optimal duty and slices pair can damage the amplifier
            (datasheet 7.4.1).

    config MESH_SAFE_POWER_CDBM
        int "The safe power: every transmit until the app confirms the antennas, hundredths of a dBm"
        range -1000 450
        default 0
        help
            The module ships with its antennas loose, and the LR2021's
            amplifiers are guaranteed only into a load (datasheet 3-2: up to a
            VSWR of 10:1; an open SMA is an infinite one). The chip cannot tell a
            missing antenna, so the SDK does not guess: until
            the app says the antennas are on (mesh_radio_antennas_confirm(true)),
            every transmit on both bands is held to this power, and a band whose
            amplifier tripped its over-current alarm is held to it again until
            the app confirms once more. The status says so (power_capped,
            antenna_suspected).

            0 dBm by default. Raise it only with an antenna or a 50 ohm load
            fitted. It only lowers: an app that asks for less gets what it
            asks. The top of the range is the 2.4 GHz band's cap, so the safe
            power is never above the region's cap on either band. MESH_LAB
            does not lift it.

endmenu
